1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,285
Hi, and thanks for taking the time.

2
00:00:03,435 --> 00:00:09,331
Today I'm going to show you what came out of our pilot project, called Wind Intelligence.

3
00:00:09,482 --> 00:00:13,106
It's a web portal, built for your day-ahead buying team.

4
00:00:14,709 --> 00:00:17,104
Let's start with the problem you face.

5
00:00:17,256 --> 00:00:21,979
Every day, your team has to decide how much power to buy for tomorrow.

6
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:29,036
Wind is a big part of Britain's power, and it changes hour by hour. So you need a wind forecast.

7
00:00:29,193 --> 00:00:32,170
But new forecasts come out many times a day.

8
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:36,704
So which one could your team really use, at the moment they decide?

9
00:00:36,859 --> 00:00:40,162
And how good are these forecasts? Can you trust them?

10
00:00:41,761 --> 00:00:47,533
In this pilot, we answered both questions, using real public data from Elexon.

11
00:00:47,684 --> 00:00:55,137
We checked 768 hours, over 32 days, against 280 forecast runs.

12
00:00:55,291 --> 00:01:00,855
We used just two data sets, for decisions made in January 2026.

13
00:01:04,404 --> 00:01:07,482
Here's the portal. This is the Decision view.

14
00:01:07,634 --> 00:01:12,381
Your team picks a decision time. The default is 09:20, UK time,

15
00:01:12,381 --> 00:01:16,751
on the day before. That's when a day-ahead auction closes.

16
00:01:16,907 --> 00:01:22,239
Then they pick the day they're buying for. Let's take Monday, the 12th of January.

17
00:01:22,393 --> 00:01:28,265
The big number is the forecast that your team would have seen: 19.3 gigawatts,

18
00:01:28,265 --> 00:01:33,686
on average. And it only uses what had been published by 09:20 on Sunday.

19
00:01:33,846 --> 00:01:40,275
Now look at this tile: wind to count on. It says, plan with 15 gigawatts, not 19.

20
00:01:40,275 --> 00:01:46,784
In similar past hours, wind came in at or above this level, nine times out of ten.

21
00:01:46,944 --> 00:01:51,994
The row below shows what really happened. Wind averaged 14.

22
00:01:51,994 --> 00:01:57,043
4 gigawatts. So the forecast was too high, in all 24 hours.

23
00:01:57,198 --> 00:02:02,123
This was one of the worst days of the month, so it shows the problem clearly.

24
00:02:02,123 --> 00:02:07,112
Even the safe number was a bit high, but it was much closer than the forecast.

25
00:02:08,670 --> 00:02:12,730
The chart tells the same story. Blue is the forecast.

26
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:17,403
Orange is the real wind. And the pink area is a normal range.

27
00:02:17,559 --> 00:02:21,379
If you hover over any hour, you can see the exact numbers.

28
00:02:22,313 --> 00:02:26,684
Every chart also has a table view, for people who like numbers.

29
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:31,838
Your team can also see how the forecast moved during the day, run by run.

30
00:02:32,329 --> 00:02:39,586
And if the team decides at 09:50 instead, the portal picks a newer forecast, and everything updates.

31
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:44,763
Now for the second question: how good is the forecast?

32
00:02:44,913 --> 00:02:49,288
Over the whole month, the forecast was too high by 1.

33
00:02:49,288 --> 00:02:53,828
8 gigawatts on average. That's 16% of the average wind.

34
00:02:53,985 --> 00:02:59,973
It was too high in 85% of the hours, and on 28 of the 32 days.

35
00:03:00,128 --> 00:03:04,936
Red bars mean the forecast was too high, and most of the bars are red.

36
00:03:05,461 --> 00:03:09,432
The gap is biggest when the forecast is big. At 17.

37
00:03:09,432 --> 00:03:13,948
8 gigawatts or more, it was too high in every single hour.

38
00:03:14,099 --> 00:03:17,153
The forecast still beats a simple guess, like:

39
00:03:17,153 --> 00:03:22,000
tomorrow will look like right now. Its typical miss is about half as big.

40
00:03:22,155 --> 00:03:26,746
So the forecast is useful, but it leans high. On this month's data,

41
00:03:26,746 --> 00:03:31,885
fixing just the average gap would shrink the typical miss by about a third.

42
00:03:33,446 --> 00:03:38,339
The Method page explains every rule in plain words, and lists the limits.

43
00:03:38,490 --> 00:03:44,404
This is one winter month, from one source. And the portal has no demand or price data,

44
00:03:44,404 --> 00:03:48,736
so it tells you how much wind to count on, not how much to buy.

45
00:03:50,376 --> 00:03:54,992
So, in short, the portal shows the newest forecast your team could use,

46
00:03:54,992 --> 00:03:59,608
shows how wrong it has been, and gives you a safer number to plan with.

47
00:03:59,762 --> 00:04:04,353
Good next steps are more months of data, then demand and price data,

48
00:04:04,353 --> 00:04:07,324
and finding out why the forecast leans high.

49
00:04:07,478 --> 00:04:10,232
Now, part two is for the technical team.

50
00:04:13,294 --> 00:04:15,264
Part two is for the tech lead.

51
00:04:15,414 --> 00:04:18,869
I'll cover the architecture, the design, the analysis,

52
00:04:18,869 --> 00:04:21,877
the code, and how we work. I'll keep it simple.

53
00:04:23,491 --> 00:04:25,207
Here's the architecture.

54
00:04:25,358 --> 00:04:30,340
The front end is React with TypeScript. It shows the pages you just saw.

55
00:04:30,492 --> 00:04:35,805
It calls a Python back end, built with FastAPI. There's no database,

56
00:04:35,805 --> 00:04:39,478
and in production only two packages are listed.

57
00:04:39,635 --> 00:04:45,319
The back end reads one data file: a saved copy of January from Elexon.

58
00:04:45,319 --> 00:04:51,733
January is in the past, so a file is fast, and it works even if Elexon is down.

59
00:04:51,892 --> 00:04:57,863
A script called ingest can rebuild that file. It stops with an error if there's a gap,

60
00:04:57,863 --> 00:05:01,751
a conflicting duplicate, or a row it would have to drop.

61
00:05:03,362 --> 00:05:05,335
This is the key design rule.

62
00:05:05,488 --> 00:05:09,382
Each day, Elexon publishes about eight forecast runs.

63
00:05:09,535 --> 00:05:16,475
For each hour of the delivery day, we take the newest run published at or before the decision time.

64
00:05:16,475 --> 00:05:20,260
The 09:30 run is ten minutes late, so we don't use it.

65
00:05:20,418 --> 00:05:23,975
This is the real function. It finds the latest run,

66
00:05:23,975 --> 00:05:27,880
at or before the cutoff, that has a value for that hour.

67
00:05:28,034 --> 00:05:32,613
Error is forecast minus actual, so positive means too high.

68
00:05:32,613 --> 00:05:36,105
Forecasts are hourly and wind is half-hourly,

69
00:05:36,105 --> 00:05:41,692
so we match start times. Other choices change the error by less than 2%.

70
00:05:43,298 --> 00:05:44,796
Now, the analysis.

71
00:05:44,948 --> 00:05:49,801
The pink range comes from past errors. We group forecasts by size,

72
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:55,904
keep the middle 80% of the errors in each group, and blend smoothly between groups.

73
00:05:56,061 --> 00:06:02,231
The day being viewed is never used to build its own range. As a check,

74
00:06:02,231 --> 00:06:08,753
an 80% range should hold about 80% of outcomes. In our data, it holds 78%.

75
00:06:08,913 --> 00:06:13,912
We also compare with a simple guess: keep the last known wind the same.

76
00:06:13,912 --> 00:06:17,433
The forecast's typical error is about half as big.

77
00:06:17,588 --> 00:06:20,023
The main limit is that it's one month,

78
00:06:20,023 --> 00:06:24,124
and the range uses later days, so it doesn't promise the future.

79
00:06:29,085 --> 00:06:36,191
The API is easy to explore. FastAPI builds this documentation page by itself.

80
00:06:36,346 --> 00:06:40,765
There are four endpoints: health, meta, decision, and accuracy.

81
00:06:41,642 --> 00:06:44,632
Health returns a fingerprint of the data file,

82
00:06:44,632 --> 00:06:48,466
so we can check that the live site is using the right data.

83
00:06:48,616 --> 00:06:54,294
The decision and accuracy endpoints take the decision time as an input.

84
00:06:54,294 --> 00:07:01,892
Bad inputs get a clear error: 404 for a day outside the January window, and 422 for a bad time.

85
00:07:03,573 --> 00:07:05,078
Now, the code base.

86
00:07:05,230 --> 00:07:11,006
backend/app/wind holds the Python. The scope file sets the January window.

87
00:07:11,006 --> 00:07:18,187
Elexon and ingest fetch and check the data. Analytics has the maths, and routes has the API.

88
00:07:18,347 --> 00:07:24,737
frontend/src has the views and the hand-written charts. data/elexon has the snapshot.

89
00:07:24,737 --> 00:07:30,826
And the docs folder and the files at the top record every decision and every bug.

90
00:07:30,984 --> 00:07:35,987
To change the time window, start in the scope file, then run ingest again.

91
00:07:35,987 --> 00:07:41,936
To add a number, add it in analytics, then in schemas and routes, and then in the views.

92
00:07:43,548 --> 00:07:45,378
Now, the key practices.

93
00:07:45,529 --> 00:07:49,603
We have 219 Python tests, and 19 front-end tests.

94
00:07:49,603 --> 00:07:55,755
Many of them recalculate the numbers from the raw data, in a separate way.

95
00:07:55,914 --> 00:08:00,824
We also broke the code on purpose, in eight ways. The tests caught all eight.

96
00:08:00,824 --> 00:08:04,395
One slipped through at first, so we added a test for it.

97
00:08:04,552 --> 00:08:09,433
19 browser checks click through the real app, including the live site.

98
00:08:09,433 --> 00:08:13,686
Versions are pinned, and decisions and bugs are written down.

99
00:08:15,294 --> 00:08:21,271
To run it, use docker compose up. The site opens on port 8080.

100
00:08:21,422 --> 00:08:25,416
In production, the React build is served as static files,

101
00:08:25,416 --> 00:08:28,709
and the Python app runs as one Vercel function.

102
00:08:28,864 --> 00:08:33,196
Two honest notes. The Vercel project isn't linked to GitHub,

103
00:08:33,196 --> 00:08:38,538
so a push doesn't redeploy it. And there's no automatic test pipeline yet.

104
00:08:40,148 --> 00:08:43,574
To finish, the limits: one month, one data source,

105
00:08:43,574 --> 00:08:48,097
no demand or price, and we don't know why the forecast leans high.

106
00:08:48,250 --> 00:08:52,839
This project was built with an AI coding assistant, Claude Code,

107
00:08:52,839 --> 00:08:57,572
from Anthropic. And the voice in this video is computer generated.

108
00:08:57,729 --> 00:08:59,142
Thanks for watching.

